# 3. Define the product contract before coding

The primary request object should be approximately:

```ts
interface NamingRequest {
  description: string

  audience: Array<
    "consumer" |
    "business" |
    "developer" |
    "ai_agent"
  >

  constraints: {
    maxPurchasePrice?: number
    maxRenewalPrice?: number
    premiumAllowed: boolean

    tlds?: string[]

    minLength?: number
    maxLength?: number

    hyphensAllowed?: boolean
    numbersAllowed?: boolean
  }

  namingPreference: {
    semanticPrecision: number
    brandElasticity: number
    memorability: number
    agentDiscoverability: number
  }
}
```

So the agent request:

> Find the best domain under $20.

compiles to:

```text
maxPurchasePrice = 20
```

Not:

```text
cheap domains get a slightly better score
```

That's critical.

## Hard constraints first

Define:

[
F = {
d:
purchasable(d)
\land purchasePrice(d)\le B
\land renewalPrice(d)\le R
\land TLD(d)\in T
\land premium(d)\in P
}
]

Only candidates in (F) reach expensive evaluation.

That means if:

```text
amazingname.com    $4,000
prettygood.dev     $14
goodthing.io       $18
weaker.xyz          $3
```

and the budget is $20:

`amazingname.com` simply does not exist in the optimization problem.

This is exactly where the name.com API becomes essential rather than decorative. Search exposes `purchasable`, `premium`, `purchasePrice`, `purchaseType` and `renewalPrice`. ([name.com Core API][3])

