# 11. Stage B — Semantic Inversion

This is the signature algorithm.

Evaluator sees only:

```text
factmole.dev
```

Not the project brief.

Instead of free-form interpretation alone, construct a blinded classification task.

Example:

```text
Which functions would you most expect
factmole.dev to provide?

A. Fact verification
B. Image generation
C. Database hosting
D. Research/search
E. Payroll
F. Evidence retrieval
G. Email marketing
H. Gaming
```

Choices are randomized.

Target:

```text
A / D / F
```

Then:

[
SemanticTransmission(d)
=======================

P(\text{intended concepts selected}\mid d)
]

Do it across multiple evaluator families.

This is far more defensible than:

> GPT says 9.4/10.

---

# 12. Stage C — Pairwise tournament

Now show the actual product description and two domain candidates:

```text
Product:
"API that checks factual claims
against primary sources."

Which is the stronger domain?

A: factmole.dev
B: proofhound.dev
```

Run both:

```text
AB
BA
```

to kill position bias.

Use AgentSEOLab's pairwise raw-observation philosophy.

Then fit a **Bradley–Terry** model:

[
P(i \succ j)
============

\frac{e^{\beta_i}}
{e^{\beta_i}+e^{\beta_j}}
]

So instead of:

```text
FactMole 8.7
ProofHound 8.3
```

we get:

```text
Estimated pairwise strength

FactMole      1.31
ProofHound    0.82
SourceFox     0.41
```

with uncertainty.

Much more meaningful.

